---
import BaseLayout from "../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import DocsSidebar from "../../components/DocsSidebar.astro";
import ModelPickerDemo from "../../components/demos/ModelPickerDemo";
import ModelPickerPlayground from "../../components/demos/ModelPickerPlayground";
import ApiTable from "../../components/ApiTable.astro";
---

<BaseLayout title="Model Picker" withSidebar>
  <DocsSidebar slot="sidebar" />
  <div class="docs-prose">
    <h1>Model Picker</h1>
    <p>
      Choose the model a chat is sent to, from a
      <a href="/components/composer/">Composer</a>'s toolbar. A compact pill
      <code>&lt;button&gt;</code> opens a <code>role="listbox"</code> in a
      native popover, with provider groups, descriptions, badges, logos,
      disabled options and typeahead.
    </p>

    <div class="docs-example">
      <ModelPickerDemo client:visible />
    </div>

    <h2>Playground</h2>
    <p>Toggle every adjustable prop and watch the component — and the code — update live.</p>
    <ModelPickerPlayground client:visible />

    <h2>Props</h2>
    <ApiTable slug="model-picker" />
    <p>
      Each option is <code>{"{ value, label, description?, group?, badges?, icon?, disabled? }"}</code>.
      Consecutive options with the same <code>group</code> are listed under one
      heading. A badge is a string (a neutral <a href="/components/badge/">Badge</a>)
      or <code>{"{ label, variant }"}</code>.
    </p>

    <h2>Why not Select?</h2>
    <p>
      A native <code>&lt;select&gt;</code> is the right answer for plain
      options, and <a href="/components/select/">Select</a> is one. A model
      list isn't plain: each option carries a description, badges and a logo,
      and <code>&lt;option&gt;</code> can only hold text outside Chromium's
      <code>appearance: base-select</code>. So the picker keeps what the
      platform does give — the popover's top layer, light dismiss, Escape and
      focus return to the trigger — and adds the WAI-ARIA listbox pattern by
      hand.
    </p>

    <h2>In a composer</h2>
    <p>
      The trigger is sized to <code>--kernel-control-height-sm</code>, level
      with a small send button, and drawn with Kernel's optical pill recipe:
      the radius stops at a true half-height pill, the text clears the end
      caps, and the logo and chevron sit concentric with them at every
      radius setting. It opens upward by default, since a composer sits at the
      bottom of the screen, and flips when there's no room.
    </p>
    <pre class="docs-pre"><code>{`<Composer
  actionsLeading={<ModelPicker options={models} value={model} onValueChange={setModel} />}
  actionsTrailing={({ submit }) => <Button size="sm" variant="primary" onClick={submit}>Send</Button>}
/>`}</code></pre>
    <p>
      In the elements package, options come from the <code>options</code>
      property, or from light-DOM <code>&lt;option&gt;</code>s (optionally in
      <code>&lt;optgroup label&gt;</code>) with <code>data-description</code>
      and <code>data-badges="New:accent, Fast"</code>. It fires
      <code>valuechange</code> with <code>detail.value</code>.
    </p>

    <h2>Keyboard</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><kbd>Enter</kbd>, <kbd>Space</kbd>, <kbd>↓</kbd> or <kbd>↑</kbd> on the trigger opens the list on the current model.</li>
      <li><kbd>↓</kbd>/<kbd>↑</kbd> move through enabled options; <kbd>Home</kbd>/<kbd>End</kbd> jump to the ends.</li>
      <li><kbd>Enter</kbd> or <kbd>Space</kbd> selects and returns focus to the trigger; <kbd>Escape</kbd> closes without changing it; <kbd>Tab</kbd> closes and moves on.</li>
      <li>Typing jumps to a model by name — on the open list or the closed trigger. Repeating one letter cycles through the models that start with it.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Accessibility</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>The trigger reads "Model, Atlas Swift" — the model name, with a visually hidden prefix (<code>labels.model</code>) saying what it is.</li>
      <li>Focus moves to the list and <code>aria-activedescendant</code> tracks the active option, so a screen reader announces each option as you arrow.</li>
      <li>Groups are <code>role="group"</code> labelled by their heading, so crossing into a provider announces it.</li>
      <li>Disabled options stay listed and announced as unavailable (<code>aria-disabled</code>), and are skipped by the arrows and typeahead.</li>
      <li>Logos are decorative; the name already says which model it is.</li>
    </ul>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
